>
  <p>Portable air conditioners can be useful when a permanent split system is not practical, particularly for renters, apartments or rooms that only need occasional cooling.</p>
  <p>But they are not simply air conditioners on wheels.</p>
  <p>A portable refrigerated air conditioner still needs to move heat outside, usually through an exhaust hose fitted to a window. The room, window, hose position, noise and condensate arrangement can all affect whether the unit is practical.</p>
  <p><strong>Before buying one, check the room and the window first.</strong></p>
  <p>For a broader comparison of fixed, portable and evaporative systems, start with the <a href="/guides/air-conditioning-buying-guide/">Air Conditioning Buying Guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Make sure it is actually an air conditioner</h2>
  <p>A portable refrigerated air conditioner removes heat from the room and exhausts it outside. A portable evaporative cooler passes air through water and adds moisture.</p>
  <p>They are not interchangeable: evaporative cooling is best suited to hot, dry conditions, while adding moisture can make it a poor substitute in humid coastal, tropical and subtropical areas.</p>
  <p>Be cautious when products are described simply as an <strong>air cooler</strong> rather than an air conditioner. Our guide comparing <a href="/guides/evaporative-cooler-vs-refrigerated-air-conditioning/">evaporative coolers and refrigerated air conditioning</a> explains the climate and ventilation differences.</p>

  <h2>Check the window before you buy</h2>
  <p>Most portable refrigerated air conditioners need an exhaust hose connected to the outside.</p>
  <p>That usually means fitting a supplied window kit into a sliding or sash window.</p>
  <p>Before buying, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>how the window opens</li>
    <li>the dimensions of the opening</li>
    <li>whether the supplied kit will fit</li>
    <li>whether extra sealing will be required</li>
    <li>whether the window can still be secured</li>
    <li>where the unit can sit while connected to the window.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Awning, casement and unusual window designs may require a different solution from a standard sliding-window kit. Renters should also check the lease and seek permission where required before fixing or altering anything.</p>
  <p>A portable air conditioner is much less useful if there is nowhere practical to exhaust the hot air.</p>

  <h2>Keep the exhaust hose short and sensible</h2>
  <p>The exhaust hose carries hot air from the air conditioner to the outside.</p>
  <p>Follow the manufacturer’s installation requirements and avoid unnecessarily extending, crushing or sharply bending the hose.</p>
  <p>A long or poorly routed hose creates more hot surface area inside the room and can make the installation less effective.</p>
  <p>The unit also needs enough space around it for airflow, so squeezing it tightly behind furniture simply because that is where the window is located may not work well.</p>

  <h2>Understand the limitation of single-hose units</h2>
  <p>Many portable air conditioners sold for household use have a single exhaust hose.</p>
  <p>The unit takes air from inside the room, uses some of it to carry heat away and exhausts that air outside.</p>
  <p>This reduces the air pressure in the room, encouraging warmer air from adjoining rooms or outside to enter through gaps and openings.</p>
  <p>This is one reason a portable unit can struggle more than expected in a large, poorly sealed or open-plan space.</p>
  <p>Portable air conditioners generally make the most sense in <strong>smaller, enclosed rooms</strong> where doors and windows can be kept closed while cooling.</p>

  <h2>Treat claimed room size as a starting point</h2>
  <p>A manufacturer’s suggested room area can be useful, but the same room-sizing considerations that apply to fixed air conditioners still matter.</p>
  <p>Cooling demand can increase with:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>strong afternoon sun</li>
    <li>north- or west-facing glazing</li>
    <li>large windows</li>
    <li>poor insulation</li>
    <li>high ceilings</li>
    <li>upstairs rooms</li>
    <li>open adjoining spaces</li>
    <li>heat-producing appliances.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A small shaded bedroom and a similarly sized west-facing upstairs room can place very different demands on an air conditioner.</p>
  <p>Use our guide to <a href="/guides/what-size-air-conditioner-do-i-need/">air-conditioner sizing</a> as a starting point. If the room gains excessive heat through the roof or walls, also check the practical options in our <a href="/guides/home-insulation-basics/">home insulation guide</a>.</p>

