  <h2>Portable does not mean quiet</h2>
  <p>The compressor, fan and other major components of a portable air conditioner are inside the room with you.</p>
  <p>That makes noise particularly important if the unit will be used in a bedroom, study or television room.</p>
  <p>Do not rely only on terms such as <strong>sleep mode</strong>, <strong>quiet mode</strong> or <strong>low noise</strong>.</p>
  <p>Check published noise figures where available, remembering that fan speed and compressor operation can change the sound while the unit is running.</p>
  <p>CHOICE testing has found that even some quieter portable units remain clearly audible. For a bedroom, consider noise before purchase rather than discovering it on the first hot night.</p>

  <h2>Check how condensate is handled</h2>
  <p>Refrigerated air conditioning removes moisture from the air as it cools.</p>
  <p>Depending on the design and operating conditions, a portable unit may evaporate some condensate through the exhaust system, collect water internally or require a drain hose.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>whether a tank needs emptying</li>
    <li>whether continuous drainage is supported</li>
    <li>where a drain hose could go</li>
    <li>what happens when the tank becomes full.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This deserves particular attention in humid climates, where the unit may need to remove more moisture from the indoor air.</p>

  <h2>Dry mode can be useful in humid weather</h2>
  <p>Many portable air conditioners include a <strong>dry</strong> or <strong>dehumidify</strong> mode.</p>
  <p>This can be useful when humidity is uncomfortable but heavy cooling is not required. Our guide to <a href="/guides/air-conditioner-dry-mode-humidity/">air-conditioner dry mode and humidity</a> explains when it suits the conditions.</p>
  <p>It does not necessarily replace a dedicated dehumidifier, and operation differs between models, so check whether dry mode requires continuous drainage and how the manufacturer intends it to be used.</p>

  <h2>Think about how portable it really is</h2>
  <p>Portable air conditioners commonly have wheels, but many are still large and heavy.</p>
  <p>Moving one from room to room may also mean moving the exhaust hose, window fittings, drainage arrangement and power connection.</p>
  <p>Check the unit’s dimensions and weight before buying, and consider where it will be stored when summer is over.</p>
  <p>For many households, “portable” means <strong>movable when necessary</strong>, not something that is conveniently wheeled between bedrooms every evening.</p>

  <h2>When is a portable air conditioner a good choice?</h2>
  <p>A portable refrigerated air conditioner can make sense when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>you rent and cannot install a fixed system</li>
    <li>strata or property restrictions prevent permanent installation</li>
    <li>only one small room needs occasional cooling</li>
    <li>you need a temporary cooling solution</li>
    <li>installing a split system is not practical.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If permanent installation is possible, <a href="/guides/portable-vs-window-wall-vs-split-system-air-conditioner/">compare portable, window/wall and split-system air conditioners</a> before deciding.</p>
  <p>Fixed systems generally avoid the portable unit’s flexible exhaust hose and keep more of the refrigeration equipment outside the occupied room. Our guide to <a href="/guides/split-system-air-conditioner-installation-costs/">split-system installation costs</a> explains the site and quote questions to check first.</p>

  <h2>Before you buy</h2>
  <p>Check the room, window and exhaust route before comparing portable air conditioners.</p>
  <p>Then consider cooling capacity, noise, condensate handling, dry mode, physical size and the realities of your local climate.</p>
  <p>A portable air conditioner can solve a genuine cooling problem, particularly where permanent installation is not possible.</p>
  <p>Even a capable portable unit is a poor purchase if the window cannot accommodate it, the room is too demanding or the noise makes it unsuitable for how you intend to use it.</p>

